{
  "slug": "genero-trampas",
  "number": 27,
  "part": 7,
  "level": "A2",
  "title": "Tricky genders",
  "tagline": "Le sourire ends in -e, la mer doesn't: the words that break the pattern.",
  "goal": "Recognise everyday words whose gender breaks the usual pattern, use word endings as clues, and tell apart the words whose meaning changes with their gender.",
  "minutes": 10,
  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "cambian",
      "title": "Words that break the pattern",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In [[2]] you saw the rough rule: words ending in **-e** are often feminine, the rest often masculine. Many common words break it, so it's worth learning them with their article."
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": ["Masculine, but ends in -e", "Feminine, but no final -e"],
          "rows": [
            ["*{le} sourire* (smile)", "*{la} couleur* (colour)"],
            ["*{le} musée* (museum)", "*{la} mer* (sea)"],
            ["*{le} lycée* (high school)", "*{la} dent* (tooth)"],
            ["*{le} silence*", "*{la} fin* (end)"],
            ["*{le} modèle* (model)", "*{la} peur* (fear)"],
            ["*{le} groupe* (group)", "*{la} main* (hand)"],
            ["*{le} rêve* (dream)", "*{la} nuit* (night)"],
            ["*{le} visage* (face)", "*{la} souris* (mouse)"]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "tip",
          "title": "Learn them with an adjective",
          "text": "A short phrase sticks better than a list: *du lait {frais}, une couleur {claire}, la mer {froide}, un beau sourire*."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "terminaciones",
      "title": "Clues in the ending",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": ["Ending", "Gender", "Examples", "Watch out"],
          "rows": [
            ["*-eur* (qualities, feelings)", "feminine", "*la couleur, la chaleur, la douleur, l'odeur, l'erreur*", "exceptions: *le bonheur, le malheur, l'honneur*"],
            ["*-eur* (people, machines)", "masculine", "*le chanteur, l'ordinateur, le moteur*", ""],
            ["*-age*", "masculine", "*le voyage, le fromage, le message*", "exceptions: *la plage, la page, l'image* (f.)"],
            ["*-ème*", "masculine", "*le problème, le système, le thème*", "they end in -e but are masculine"],
            ["*-tion, -sion, -té*", "feminine", "*la décision, la liberté, la beauté*", "almost always"]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "note",
          "text": "With *l'* you can't see the gender: *l'image, l'ordinateur*. To check, think of *une image*, *un ordinateur*."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "dos-generos",
      "title": "One gender, one meaning",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Some words exist in both genders, with different meanings:"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": ["Masculine", "Feminine"],
          "rows": [
            ["*le livre* = the book", "*la livre* = the pound (half a kilo, money)"],
            ["*le tour* = the tour, the turn", "*la tour* = the tower"],
            ["*le poste* = the job, the position", "*la poste* = the post office"],
            ["*le mode* = the mode, the method", "*la mode* = fashion"],
            ["*le manche* = the handle", "*la manche* = the sleeve"]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "examples",
          "items": [
            { "fr": "On fait {le} tour de {la} tour Eiffel ?", "tr": "Shall we walk around the Eiffel Tower?" }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
